<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Labyrinth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Labyrinth, in Greek mythology, was the maze built by the craftsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daedalus">Daedalus</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> to hold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a>. The hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a> killed the monster there and found his way out with the thread given to him by Minos's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a>. Ancient writers placed it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, and the complex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> palace excavated there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> has often been linked with the legend.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/labyrinth.jpg" alt="Labyrinth" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlMare · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_silver_coin_400bc.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Labyrinth is a word of pre-Greek origin whose derivation is uncertain. Maximillian Mayer suggested in 1892 that it came from labrys, a Lydian word for a double-bladed axe, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> took this to mean the house of the double axe. That link lost ground after the deciphering of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> in the 1950s, when a Mycenaean Greek form, da-pu₂-ri-to, appeared. It may be related to the Minoan word du-pu₂-re, found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> on libation tablets and connected with Mount Dikte and Mount Ida, both associated with caverns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Evans believed the complexity of the palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, with its bull motifs and carved labrys signs, showed it to be the Labyrinth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daedalus">Daedalus</a>. On the strength of a passage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, it has also been suggested that the palace held a dancing-ground made for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a> by Daedalus. In the 2000s the geographer Nicholas Howarth called Evans's view something to be treated sceptically. His team found that the Skotino cave was natural, and another candidate is a series of partly human-made tunnels at Gortyn.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> applied the word to a building complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> near the City of Crocodiles, which he thought surpassed the pyramids. Its remains were found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawara">Hawara</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> by Flinders Petrie.</p><p>As a pattern, the single-path seven-course Classical design appears on Cretan coins from about 430 BCE, even though the myth clearly describes a branching maze in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a> was trapped. Depictions from the Roman era to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> are almost always of this single-path kind, and many scholars now use labyrinth for it and maze for a branching puzzle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> made many decorative labyrinths in mosaic, often with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a> at the centre. Medieval examples followed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> pattern, the commonest, appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> as early as the 9th century. The great pavement labyrinths of Chartres, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> date from the twelfth to fourteenth centuries. Some 500 or more labyrinths of stone were also laid out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, often named Troy Town, perhaps to trap malevolent trolls or winds for fishing communities.</p><p>Similar designs appear in other cultures, among them the Tohono O'odham, whose pattern features I'itoi, the Man in the Maze. From the late 20th century labyrinths have been revived, and about 6,000 are registered with the Worldwide Labyrinth Locator.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Labyrinth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Classical Knossos put the <b>Labyrinth</b> on its silver coins, sometimes with the Minotaur, centuries after the Bronze Age palace that inspired the legend had fallen into ruin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the New Kingdom Senusret III was worshipped as a god in Nubia, and Greek writers knew Amenemhat III's mortuary temple at Hawara as the <b>Labyrinth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greeks remembered Bronze Age Crete in myth: Minos the lawgiver king, the <b>Labyrinth</b> built by Daedalus, and the Minotaur killed by Theseus with the help of Ariadne.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
